The technical choice is rarely yours to make, but its consequences are yours to live with: website speed, maintenance bills, security, and the ability to change a page without calling someone. Here are the differences that really matter.

## Speed: the most visible gap

A Next.js website serves pre-generated pages, with no database to query on each visit. A WordPress website builds the page on demand, then compensates with caching plugins. On a phone on average 4G, the usual situation in Morocco, the gap shows: a few hundred milliseconds against two to four seconds for a WordPress site loaded with plugins.

A well-optimized WordPress site, properly hosted and trimmed down, gets good scores. The problem isn't WordPress but what gets added to it: every plugin installed slows every page.

## Security and upkeep

WordPress runs a large share of the web, which makes it the most attacked target. Break-ins almost always come through an outdated plugin, not the core software. A WordPress website therefore needs real upkeep: updates, backups, monitoring.

A Next.js website published as static pages has no exposed database and no public admin panel: the attack surface is almost nil, and there are no plugins to update every month.

## Editing independence: WordPress's advantage

This is where WordPress keeps a clear lead. You add a page, change a photo, move a block, publish an article, without anyone. For a business that publishes several times a week, that's decisive.

A Next.js website can offer the same by connecting a content management system, but it's a component to plan from the start, with its cost. Without it, every change goes through a developer, acceptable for a ten-page website that rarely changes, painful for a news site.

## Cost, at build time and over three years

{% table %}
* 
* WordPress
* Next.js
---
* Build (business website)
* Often cheaper
* Similar or higher
---
* Yearly hosting
* MAD 600 – 3,000
* MAD 0 – 1,200
---
* Monthly upkeep
* Required
* Minimal
---
* Content changes
* You, at no cost
* Developer, or a CMS to plan
---
* Redesign after 3 years
* Common (theme ages)
* Rare (code is yours)
{% /table %}

Over three years the gap narrows: what you save at build time with WordPress, you pay back in hosting, maintenance and paid plugins.

## Which one, depending on your situation

- Blog, magazine, news website updated every day: WordPress.
- Company business website, service website, ad landing page: Next.js, for speed and peace of mind.
- Standard online store run in-house: WooCommerce or Shopify, rather than a custom build.
- Business platform, client area, web application: Next.js, because WordPress isn't made for that.
- Very tight budget and a need to publish yourself: WordPress, keeping plugins to a minimum.

## What we use, and why

Wevtex builds with Next.js because most of our clients want a website that loads fast on mobile, runs without monthly upkeep and belongs entirely to them, code included. This website is an example: every page is pre-generated and served as is, in French and in English.

When a client needs to publish every day, we say so and connect a content management system, or we recommend WordPress. The right tool is the one that fits the real use, not the one the provider prefers.
